body:
Glaser's perfect start is over. Ainsworth won the #1 vs. #2 game 99.34-73.18 behind Kenneth Walker III (30.4) and Zay Flowers (21.8), and the 73.18 was the lowest score of the week. Jaxon Smith-Njigba followed his 30.4 with 10.1, and Glaser started Dalton Schultz at tight end again (1.9). Ainsworth could afford to leave Romeo Doubs (20.8) on the bench behind Parker Washington (1.5). Ainsworth now leads the all-time series 13-12 and the league in scoring with 482.3 points. Glaser is 3-1 after four weeks for the second straight year: last season's Week 4 was a 126.54-102.88 loss to Hagan.

{{visual:week4-scores}}

That makes it a four-way tie at 3-1: Ainsworth, Glaser, Goetz, and Larson. From 2013-2025, 39 teams started 3-1. 29 of them made the playoffs and 8 won the title. Goetz is the surprise of the group. He started 0-4 last year and finished 12th. This week he put up the high score, 138.78, with Chuba Hubbard (25.4) and Emanuel Wilson (25.5, from the flex), and he's 2nd in the power rankings. One caveat: Goetz has played the easiest schedule in the league so far, 12th of 12.

Pfaffinger is 0-4. He lost 138.78-120.56 even though Tetairoa McMillan scored 38.2, the most by any rostered player this week. Alvin Kamara scored 20.3 on the bench while Bhayshul Tuten (10.1) started at flex, and KC Concepcion scored 8.9 on the bench while Rashee Rice started and put up 0.0. Those two swaps add up to 19.1, more than the 18.22 margin. It's Pfaffinger's second 0-4 start (2020 was the first), which puts him with Larson (2013, 2015) and Hagan (2021, 2022) as the only managers to do it twice. None of the 9 teams that started 0-4 from 2013-2025 won a title, and only 2 made the playoffs. One of those 2 was Pfaffinger's own 2020 team. It had faced 504.6 points through four weeks and still finished 5th. This year's team has faced 509.5, the 4th-most through four weeks in NWL history. Last year, Conlin and Pfaffinger both opened 5-0. This year they're a combined 1-7, and Conlin has lost three straight.

{{visual:points-against}}

Prodahl keeps picking the wrong quarterback. In Week 3 he started Drake Maye (3.8) over Kyler Murray (10.4). This week he switched to Murray, who scored 11.5, while Maye scored 26.2 on the bench. Kyle Monangai also scored 27.5 on the bench while TreVeyon Henderson scored 4.2 at flex. Those two swaps were worth 38.0 points, more than the 29.32 margin, so Prodahl was favored by 18 and lost to Zogg 121.10-91.78 anyway. CeeDee Lamb scored 32.8 for Zogg.

Hagan benched Brock Purdy again. Purdy scored 19.6 on the bench, Jalen Hurts scored 13.5 in the lineup, and over two weeks Purdy has outscored Hurts by 24.8 points without starting either game. Nothing on the bench would have closed a 114.20-75.52 loss to Larson, who got 28.8 from Javonte Williams and 22.2 from Jonathan Taylor.

{{visual:wrong-qb}}

Elsewhere: Palaia beat Muenchow 119.58-82.08 behind Bijan Robinson (27.2) and Tee Higgins (21.2) for his second straight win. Kyren Williams scored 31.7 for Muenchow, while Brian Robinson Jr. scored 25.2 on the bench and the Steelers Head Coach scored -5. Stover beat Conlin 100.72-75.62 behind Joe Burrow (23.7) and Puka Nacua (23.2), and has now won two straight. Conlin started Ladd McConkey, who scored 0.0.

Best-ball standings: what if every manager had started his best possible lineup every week, and so had every opponent? Only two games flip, both in Week 4. Prodahl's best lineup (135.56) beats Zogg's (128.40), and Pfaffinger's (139.66) edges Goetz's (138.98) by 0.68. Pfaffinger's real problem is the schedule, not the lineup. Glaser has left the most points on the bench and is 3-1 either way. Goetz has left the fewest, so best ball helps him the least. Prodahl's 43.78 left on the bench in Week 4 is the most by any team in a single week this season.

{{visual:best-ball}}

In Week 5, #1 Ainsworth plays #12 Pfaffinger, and ESPN has Pfaffinger favored by 5.5. Pfaffinger leads the all-time series 12-5 and won the last meeting, 82.06-65.06 in Week 14 of 2025. The week's two biggest spreads are both 14 points: Muenchow over Larson (Muenchow leads that series 15-3) and 1-3 Conlin over 3-1 Goetz.

Muenchow scored 168.22 and beat Larson by 86.26. That's the highest score in any NWL game since the current scoring era started in 2022, and the 7th-highest in league history. It's also Muenchow's second-best game ever, behind a 172.30 in Week 1 of 2018. Jaylen Warren led the way with 33.8, Matthew Stafford added 27.3, and Mike Evans 22.8. Every Muenchow starter other than the Head Coach (+5) scored at least 9.1. Larson never had a chance. With Patrick Mahomes on bye he started Jacoby Brissett, which worked (24.8), and he still lost by the second-biggest margin of the season.

{{visual:week5-scores}}

Ainsworth and Glaser are tied for first at 4-1. Ainsworth beat Pfaffinger 94.48-63.42. Parker Washington scored 27.4, a week after scoring 1.5, and Jordan Love started over Tyler Shough and scored 25.3 (Shough scored -0.2 on the bench). Glaser beat Hagan 120.04-88.60 behind Christian Watson (29.3), Derrick Henry (20.5), and Jaxon Smith-Njigba (20.3), and moves to #1 in the power rankings. From 2013-2025, 22 teams started 4-1. 19 of them made the playoffs and 5 won the title. Glaser has also left the most points on the bench this season (115.7), so he might still have more in him.

Pfaffinger is 0-5 for the first time in his career. His 63.42 was the lowest score of the week, with Tetairoa McMillan and Bryce Young on Carolina's bye and Rashee Rice on Kansas City's. Pfaffinger is the fifth team to start 0-5 in NWL history. None of the first four made the playoffs, and the best of them finished 9th. Pfaffinger has now faced 603.9 points, the 10th-most through five weeks in league history.

{{visual:oh-and-five}}

Prodahl finally started the right quarterback. Drake Maye scored 39.5, the top score by any rostered player in Week 5, and Prodahl beat Palaia 124.06-99.84. Palaia had Jayden Daniels (32.3) on the bench and started Jared Goff (28.9), but that wasn't the difference.

Hagan did not start Brock Purdy. For the third week in a row, Purdy outscored Jalen Hurts from the bench, this time 16.2 to 13.0. Over three weeks, that's 28.0 points left on the bench at quarterback alone. Hagan also started the Rams Head Coach, who scored -5.

{{visual:purdy-hurts}}

Elsewhere: Stover beat Zogg 111.50-69.84 behind Bucky Irving (30.5) and Puka Nacua (29.2), and has won three straight. Zogg has now scored under 70 three times in five weeks (68.50, 68.84, 69.84). CeeDee Lamb scored 1.9, and MarShawn Lloyd scored 16.2 on the bench. Conlin beat Goetz 117.50-105.60 behind Josh Allen (30.5) and Isaiah Likely (26.9), though as a 14-point favorite he didn't cover. Goetz started Brian Thomas Jr., who scored 0.0. The only other starter at zero or below was Glaser's Kenyon Sadiq (-0.7). Jahmyr Gibbs scored 35.2 for Goetz in the loss.

Best-ball standings didn't change this week. Every Week 5 winner also had the better optimal lineup. Through five weeks, four teams have a different best-ball record: Prodahl would be 3-2 and Pfaffinger 1-4, while Goetz would be 2-3 and Zogg 1-4. Goetz still leaves the fewest points on the bench (31.9). Stover left the most this week (25.8) and won anyway.

{{visual:best-ball}}

In Week 6, #4 Muenchow hosts #2 Ainsworth. Based on each team's best possible projected lineup, Ainsworth is favored by 9.5, but Muenchow leads the all-time series 12-7 and won their last meeting, 124.48-102.72 in Week 5 of 2025. Pfaffinger tries to avoid 0-6 against Conlin, who is favored by 12.5 and leads that series 11-10. Only Larson (2015) and Goetz (2025) have ever started 0-6. Cincinnati, Detroit, Miami, and Minnesota are all on bye.
